NNAAMMEE
     nnffssiioodd — local NFS asynchronous I/O server

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     nnffssiioodd [--nn _n_u_m___s_e_r_v_e_r_s]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The nnffssiioodd utility controls the maximum number of nnffssiioodd kernel processes
     which run on an NFS client machine to service asynchronous I/O requests
     to its server.  Having nnffssiioodd kernel processes improves performance but
     is not required for correct operation.

     The option is as follows:

     --nn      Specify how many processes are permitted to be started.

     Without an option, nnffssiioodd displays the current settings.  A client should
     allow enough number of processes to handle its maximum level of concur‐
     rency, typically four to six.

     If nnffssiioodd detects that the running kernel does not include NFS support,
     it will attempt to load a kernel module containing NFS code, using
     kldload(2).  If this fails, or no NFS module was available, nnffssiioodd exits
     with an error.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The nnffssiioodd utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     nfsstat(1), kldload(2), nfssvc(2), mountd(8), nfsd(8), rpcbind(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The nnffssiioodd utility first appeared in 4.4BSD.

     Starting with FreeBSD 5.0, the utility no longer starts daemons, but only
     serves as a vfs loader and sysctl(3) wrapper.

